Prompt form
-----------
Today's image models read long prompts in full; what helps is concrete, visible information, not more words.
• One English paragraph of **120–220 words**, or **60–100 words** when the picture is chiefly a figure's pose or an act (more words around a pose make the model follow it less). The first sentence is the medium followed by the request in its own words: its subject, count, pose, action and position words copied as written, not paraphrased ("arms outstretched" stays "arms outstretched", "three apples, the middle one bitten" stays exactly that), e.g. "A black-and-white documentary photograph of …". Everything after adds detail around those words; nothing after contradicts or softens them.
• The second sentence says the same thing again in concrete, drawable terms, because image models draw what is described, not what is meant. It starts with where the camera stands and what it faces, then says where each limb, hand and face is, who does what to whom, what is visible. Abstract or evaluative words ("intimate", "provocative", "violent", "humiliating") become the visible facts they stand for, and the body is named in plain anatomical words, never euphemism. E.g. "a man proposing on a bridge" → "Seen from the side at waist height: he kneels on one knee on the bridge, holding an open ring box up towards her; she stands facing him, both hands over her mouth."
• The camera is placed where, and close enough that, the point of the picture is plainly visible: the point of contact, the object in the hand, the wound. A named real person's face is large in the frame, lit and turned towards the camera, and the second sentence names the features that make it recognisable. "X as Y" means Y's body, costume or role with X's own face: say so in those words ("the bear's body with Xi Jinping's own face").
