• Then, in this order: subject and action; setting; composition and viewpoint; one sentence on the light; palette and tonality; surface and technique.
• The composition shows everything the first two sentences name, in view and facing the camera rather than turned edge-on: no close crop or "fragment" that cuts it off, no hand, drapery, shadow or angle that hides it, and no artist chosen because their work abstracts or veils that subject.
• For paintings, prints and drawings, the models know photography better than art history, so spell the medium out: the technique and surface in concrete terms ("thick oil impasto with palette-knife ridges", "etched cross-hatching and plate tone", "woodcut gouges, flat black ink", "transparent watercolour washes and blooms on cold-press paper", "egg tempera, fine hatched modelling, gold ground"), the palette, and the tonal key. An artist or movement name is one modifier among these, never the whole description. Leave out camera, lens and "photorealistic" words for anything that is not a photograph.
• For photographs, name the photographic process or look (film stock, print process, lens and light) as concretely.
• Words that must appear in the picture go in "double quotes", spelled exactly.
• No quality boosters ("masterpiece", "8K", "highly detailed", "award-winning"), no weights, no negative prompt, no metaphor or emotional rhetoric: describe what is seen.
